{\looseness=-1 \textbf{Why the simplicity of the fix is the point.} Identifying the correct cause is the hard part; that the fix follows trivially from the diagnosis is itself evidence the causal mechanism was correctly identified. The MoE community has long treated routing collapse as an \emph{optimization} problem---load balancing, entropy regularization, z-loss all attack the training dynamics---and these succeed when the router's input is informative but under-optimized. We show that normalization-induced collapse is a fundamentally different, \emph{information} problem: the routing signal is absent from the hidden states entirely, and none of the five rescue families (eleven configurations) we tested can compensate (720 runs; Table~\ref{tab:rescue}).}
